.Dd October 2, 2026
.Dt WAIT 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm waitpid ,
.Nm wait4 ,
.Nm waitid
.Nd wait for a child to change state
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/wait.h
.Ft pid_t
.Fn waitpid "pid_t pid" "int *status" "int options"
.Ft pid_t
.Fn wait4 "pid_t pid" "int *status" "int options" "struct rusage *rusage"
.Ft int
.Fn waitid "idtype_t idtype" "id_t id" "siginfo_t *infop" "int options"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn waitpid
waits for a state change in a child selected by
.Fa pid :
.Bl -tag -width "Greater than 0"
.It Greater than 0
The child with that process ID.
.It -1
Any child.
.It 0
Any child in the caller's process group.
.It Less than -1
Any child whose process group ID equals
.Ql -pid .
The value -2147483648 matches no child.
.El
.Pp
The children of a process are the children of all its threads, and any
thread may wait for any of them.
A child is a process: it has exited once its last thread has ended, and
its threads are never reported one by one.
A tracer's wait also reports the threads its process traces, as
.Xr ptrace 2
describes: their ptrace stops, a traced thread's end, and the end of a
traced process whose parent is another.
The end of a child that another process traces is the tracer's to report
first: the parent's wait passes the ended child over until the tracer's
wait has reported its end, or the tracer has ended, and the parent then
receives
.Dv SIGCHLD
and may reap it, as on Linux.
By default, the call waits for a child to exit and reaps it.
The
.Fa options
argument may combine these bits:
.Bl -tag -width "WCONTINUED (8)"
.It Dv WNOHANG No (1)
Return 0 if a matching child exists but has no reportable state change.
.It Dv WUNTRACED No (2)
Also report a stopped child.
.It Dv WCONTINUED No (8)
Also report a continued child.
.El
.Pp
Linux's
.Dv __WALL
and
.Dv __WNOTHREAD
are accepted and change nothing: every thread waits for the children of
its whole process.
.Dv __WCLONE
asks for the children that report their end with a signal other than
.Dv SIGCHLD ,
unless
.Dv __WALL
is beside it, and so selects none: every child here reports with
.Dv SIGCHLD .
Other option bits are ignored.
Stop and continue events are reported once and do not reap the child.
A stop is reported only while the child lives: a child that has ended,
or whose end has begun, reports no stop, though a stop it did not report
is still recorded.
.Pp
If
.Fa status
is not null, it receives the Linux-encoded wait status.
Use
.Dv WIFEXITED
and
.Dv WEXITSTATUS
for normal exit,
.Dv WIFSIGNALED
and
.Dv WTERMSIG
for signal death,
.Dv WIFSTOPPED
and
.Dv WSTOPSIG
for a stop, and
.Dv WIFCONTINUED
for a continuation.
.Pp
.Fn wait4
performs the same wait and accepts an optional
.Fa rusage
pointer.
For every report, an exit, a stop or a continue, the kernel fills the
72-byte i386 resource usage structure with the child's CPU time, that of
all its threads, added to that of the children the child has reaped:
.Va ru_utime
and
.Va ru_stime
are the ticks the timer charged in user mode and in the kernel, as
.Xr times 2
counts them;
.Va ru_maxrss
is the most memory in kilobytes the child or a child it reaped had in use,
as
.Xr getrusage 2
describes; and the thirteen counters after it, which the kernel does not
keep, are zero.
Reaping a child adds the same two times to the children's totals of the
caller's process.
Nonnull status and resource usage pointers are checked for write
access before the wait.
The event found is held while its report is copied out, so that no other
thread's wait takes it: a copy that fails, because another thread unmapped
or protected the memory while the caller slept, fails the call with
.Er EFAULT
and leaves the event, which a later wait reports again, so no zombie is
lost.
.Ss waitid
.Fn waitid
selects children by
.Fa idtype :
.Dv P_ALL
.Pq 0
for every child,
.Dv P_PID
.Pq 1
for the child whose process ID is
.Fa id ,
and
.Dv P_PGID
.Pq 2
for the children in process group
.Fa id ,
the caller's when
.Fa id
is 0.
.Fa options
must have at least one of the events it waits for:
.Dv WEXITED
.Pq 4
for an exit,
.Dv WSTOPPED
.Pq 2 ,
which is
.Dv WUNTRACED ,
for a stop, and
.Dv WCONTINUED
for a continue.
.Dv WNOHANG
and the three thread bits mean what they mean above, and
.Dv WNOWAIT
.Pq 0x01000000
reports an event and leaves it, so that a zombie can still be reaped and a
stop or continue reported again.
A zombie that is not waited for with
.Dv WEXITED
may still report a continue it has not reported, as on Linux.
.Pp
The event is written to the
.Vt siginfo_t
at
.Fa infop ,
when it is not null:
.Va si_signo
is
.Dv SIGCHLD ,
.Va si_pid
the child's process ID and
.Va si_uid
its real user ID, and
.Va si_code
and
.Va si_status
say what happened: an exit,
.Dv CLD_EXITED
with the exit status; a death by a signal,
.Dv CLD_KILLED ,
or
.Dv CLD_DUMPED
where the wait status says a core was dumped, with the signal; a stop,
.Dv CLD_STOPPED
with the stop signal; a continue,
.Dv CLD_CONTINUED
with
.Dv SIGCONT ;
and, for a tracer, a ptrace stop,
.Dv CLD_TRAPPED
with the stop's wait status shifted right by 8 bits: its signal, with the
event of an event stop in bits 8 to 15, as
.Xr ptrace 2
describes the status.
With
.Dv WNOHANG
and no event, those five fields and
.Va si_errno
are zero, so
.Va si_pid
0 says nothing was found.
The kernel writes only these six fields.
.Pp
The raw call takes a fifth argument, as Linux's does: a
.Vt struct rusage
pointer, which, when it is not null, receives the child's usage as
.Fn wait4
gives it when a child was reported, and is not written otherwise.
musl passes 0 there.
glibc's
.Fn posix_spawn
reaps a child that could not run its program with
.Fn waitid .
.Ss System call interface
The i386 system call numbers are 7 for
.Nm waitpid ,
114 for
.Nm wait4 ,
and 284 for
.Nm waitid .
EBX contains
.Fa pid ,
ECX the status pointer, and EDX the options.
For
.Nm wait4 ,
ESI contains the resource usage pointer.
For
.Nm waitid ,
EBX contains
.Fa idtype ,
ECX
.Fa id ,
EDX the siginfo pointer, ESI the options and EDI the resource usage
pointer.
Musl's
.Fn wait
and
.Fn waitpid
use the
.Nm wait4
system call.
.Ss Differences from Linux
A pidfd names no child here, since no descriptor is one:
.Dv P_PIDFD
.Pq 3
fails with
.Er EBADF ,
as Linux's does for a descriptor that is not a pidfd.
A wait whose status, siginfo or resource usage pointer cannot be written
fails with the child's event left in place, before the wait or after it;
Linux reaps the child and then fails.
Linux refuses unknown option bits for
.Fn waitpid
and
.Fn wait4
with
.Er EINVAL ,
where this kernel ignores them.
.Pp
x86-64 has no
.Nm waitpid :
musl's
.Fn waitpid
calls
.Nm wait4
(61), and
.Nm waitid
is 247.
The resource usage structure there is x86-64's, 144 bytes, its two timevals
and fourteen counters 64-bit longs.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn waitpid
and
.Fn wait4
return the process ID of the child whose state was reported, or 0 when
.Dv WNOHANG
is set and a matching child has no reportable state change.
.Fn waitid
returns 0 in both cases.
On failure they return -1 and set
.Va errno .
The kernel returns a negative error number on failure.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/forktest.c
forks a child that exits with status 42, waits for it, and checks
.Dv WIFEXITED
and
.Dv WEXITSTATUS .
.Pa tests/jobprobe.c
stops a child and waits with
.Dv WUNTRACED ,
continues it and waits with
.Dv WCONTINUED ,
then kills it and checks the reported signal.
.Pa tests/musl/waitidmusl.c
checks each field
.Fn waitid
fills for an exit, a death by a signal, a stop and a continue, the
resource usage it returns, what
.Dv WNOHANG
and
.Dv WNOWAIT
leave, the selection by process ID and process group, and the refusals.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[ECHILD]"
.It Bq Er EBADF
.Fn waitid
was given
.Dv P_PIDFD
with a nonnegative
.Fa id .
.It Bq Er ECHILD
No child matches
.Fa pid ,
or
.Fa idtype
and
.Fa id .
.It Bq Er EFAULT
A nonnull status, siginfo or resource usage pointer does not refer to
writable user memory; the child's event is left for a later wait.
.It Bq Er EINTR
A signal that is neither blocked nor ignored is pending while the call
would otherwise block.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
For
.Fn waitid ,
.Fa options
has a bit other than those above or none of the three events,
.Fa idtype
is unknown, or
.Fa id
is not positive for
.Dv P_PID
or is negative for
.Dv P_PGID
or
.Dv P_PIDFD .
.It Bq Er ESRCH
There is no current process.
.El
